.TH MAYI 1 "2026-09-27" "Mayi 0.3.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mayi \- submit an exact command for human approval or manage queued requests
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
mayi COMMAND [ARG ...]
mayi --queue [--after REQUEST_ID]... COMMAND [ARG ...]
mayi --wait [--fail=N] [--fail-instant] REQUEST_ID ...
mayi --status REQUEST_ID
mayi --help
mayi inspect|check COMMAND_TEXT
mayi -u USER inspect|check COMMAND_TEXT
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mayi
submits a command as a structured executable and argument vector (argv). It does not implicitly invoke a shell, expand variables, or interpret shell metacharacters. By default, the requester waits for human approval and for the command result. Queued requests return immediately but still require explicit human approval.
.PP
Approved commands run with root privileges through the separate Mayi executor. Review the exact executable and every argument before approving. The persistent interactive approval console is the
.B mayi-approve
command. Detailed requester and approver procedures are in
.BR mayi-workflow (7).
.SH COMMAND MODES
.TP
.B "mayi command [arguments ...]"
Submit one synchronous request. The command waits for an approver, then executes the stored executable/argv at most once if approved. Child stdout and stderr are returned to the requester.
.TP
.B "mayi --queue [--after request-id]... command [arguments ...]"
Submit one queued request. On successful submission, print its broker-generated request ID to standard output and exit without waiting for approval or execution. All
.B --after
options must appear before the command.
.TP
.B "mayi --wait [options] request-id..."
Observe one or more queued requests until they are terminal, or until
.B --fail-instant
exceeds the failure budget. This mode does not approve, execute, cancel, or retry jobs.
.TP
.B "mayi --status request-id"
Print the current state/result metadata for one queued request. A successful status query does not mean that the request itself succeeded.
.TP
.B "mayi inspect command-text"
Analyze the legacy restricted command-text format and print modeled operations. This is analysis only; it does not submit or execute commands.
.TP
.B "mayi check command-text"
Evaluate the legacy restricted command-text format with the advisory policy and print the result. This is analysis only; it does not execute commands or authorize the privileged executor.
.PP
The legacy analysis mode also accepts
.B -u USER
before
.B inspect
or
.B check
to display the resolved target identity. It does not change execution identity or grant authority.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --queue
Select asynchronous submission. A successful invocation prints exactly one broker-generated ID of the form
.B rq_<32 lowercase hexadecimal characters> .
No ID is printed when submission fails. Every queued request still requires approval.
.TP
.B --after REQUEST_ID
Add a dependency to the queued request. Repeat this option before the command to require multiple dependencies. It is valid only with
.B --queue .
.TP
.B --wait
Wait for and report the supplied queued request IDs. IDs must be unique and in the broker-generated format.
.TP
.B --fail=N
Set the nonnegative failure budget for
.BR --wait .
Each terminal request whose state is not
.B SUCCEEDED
uses one unit of the budget. The default is zero. For example,
.B --fail=0
allows zero unsuccessful requests; it does not set or compare a child exit status.
.TP
.B --fail-instant
Return as soon as the number of observed terminal unsuccessful requests exceeds the
.B --fail=N
budget. Other jobs continue; this option does not cancel them. IDs not observed before the early return may be printed as
.BR NOT_OBSERVED .
.TP
.B --status REQUEST_ID
Query one queued request and print its current status/result line.
.TP
.B -h , --help
Print the command usage summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.B --
End Mayi's leading command-mode parsing and submit the following executable and arguments synchronously.
.SH QUEUED EXECUTION
A successful
.B --queue
submission stores an immutable exact executable/argv operation in the broker and returns its ID. Submission is not execution and does not grant approval. Each queued operation requires explicit human approval.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi --queue /usr/bin/printf '%s\\n' 'hello'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mayi passes the format string and the word
.B hello
as arguments to
.BR /usr/bin/printf ;
the metacharacters are not interpreted by Mayi. By contrast,
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi /bin/sh -c 'some shell program'
.fi
.RE
.PP
explicitly requests a shell executable and gives it a shell program as an argument. This is broad shell execution, not ordinary argv interpreted by Mayi. The approver must inspect the exact executable and every argument.
.PP
The command may be given by absolute path or resolved by name through Mayi's fixed trusted system directories. Relative executable paths and untrusted executable locations are rejected. The complete argv, including the executable as argv[0], is bounded to 128 entries, 1,024 UTF-8 bytes per entry, and 2,048 bytes total.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Use the ID printed by one queue submission as a later request's dependency:
.PP
.nf
.RS
 d1=$(mayi --queue /usr/bin/true)
 d2=$(mayi --queue --after "$d1" /usr/bin/id)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dependencies must identify retained queued requests owned by the same authenticated requester UID. Unknown, duplicate, invalid, non-queued, cyclic, or cross-requester dependencies are rejected. Every dependent command requires its own human approval. An approved command executes only when all its dependencies have succeeded. If a dependency ends unsuccessfully, descendants are blocked and do not execute.
.SH WAITING FOR RESULTS
Use
.B --wait
to observe the chain:
.PP
.nf
.RS
d1=$(mayi --queue /usr/bin/true)
d2=$(mayi --queue --after "$d1" /usr/bin/id)
mayi --wait --fail=0 "$d1" "$d2"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The wait may remain blocked while approval is pending. A successful result line has the form:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rq_<id> SUCCEEDED mayi=0 child=0
.fi
.RE
.PP
The request state describes the queued job lifecycle. The
.B mayi=
value is Mayi's result code for that job; the
.B child=
value is the child process exit status when available, otherwise
.BR - .
For example,
.B FAILED mayi=14 child=1
means the approved command failed with child exit status 1. A
.B BLOCKED
line may include the dependency that prevented execution. A
.B DENIED
or
.B EXPLAIN_REQUESTED
request did not execute. These per-request result codes are distinct from the process exit status of the
.B mayi --wait
command.
.PP
Without
.B --fail-instant ,
.B --wait
waits until all requested IDs are terminal, prints a result line for each, and exits 1 if unsuccessful terminal requests exceed the budget; otherwise it exits 0. With
.B --fail-instant ,
it can return before every ID is observed. It never cancels the remaining work. Use
.B mayi --status REQUEST_ID
to query one ID; that command returns zero when the status query succeeds, even when the reported job state is unsuccessful.
.SH QUEUE LIFETIME
Queued requests and their stored results are broker-memory state, not durable storage. A broker restart invalidates pending queued approvals and loses retained queue results; Mayi does not restore or replay those requests. Restart-safe queue persistence is not implemented or claimed. The broker retains at most 128 request records globally and allows at most eight active requests per requester. Non-running queued requests expire after 24 hours.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Synchronous command succeeded; queue submission succeeded; help or analysis completed; a status query succeeded; or
.B --wait
finished within its failure budget.
.TP
.B 1
.B --wait
observed more unsuccessful terminal requests than the configured failure budget.
.TP
.B 2
No command was supplied, or legacy analysis input/usage was invalid.
.TP
.B 10
A synchronous request was denied by the approver. The command was not executed.
.TP
.B 11
The synchronous approval request expired.
.TP
.B 12
The request or queue options were malformed, unsupported, or outside documented bounds.
.TP
.B 13
Authorization or role checks failed.
.TP
.B 14
The approved synchronous child command failed. The child exit status is not returned as Mayi's process exit status.
.TP
.B 15
A broker or executor internal error occurred, or execution was disabled.
.TP
.B 16
Execution outcome is unknown/ambiguous, or queued status could not be reliably obtained.
.TP
.B 20
The approver requested follow-up. The command was not executed.
.PP
For queued jobs, the per-request
.B mayi=
field reports that job's Mayi result code. The exit status of
.B --wait
is separately determined by the failure budget.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
Mayi preserves the executable and argv as structured data; it does not reinterpret a command as shell text. An explicitly supplied shell, such as
.B /bin/sh -c ... ,
will run as that shell if an approver authorizes it. The approver must assess the displayed command, arguments, requester UID, and dependency relationships.
.PP
Requester and approver roles are authorized through NSS-backed groups. The broker authenticates peer UIDs with
.BR SO_PEERCRED ;
the privileged executor separately requires the exact configured broker UID. The executor revalidates the executable/argv and runs with a cleared, fixed environment. Mayi is human-gated root execution, not a command-safety analyzer or semantic allowlist.
.PP
Execution is at most once, not transactional: a command can partially change system state before failing or returning an ambiguous result. Mayi does not retry ambiguous execution. Filesystem operations remain disconnected from the privileged executor.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/mayi/request/broker.sock
Requester/broker Unix-domain socket.
.TP
.I /run/mayi/approval/broker.sock
Approver/broker Unix-domain socket used by
.BR mayi-approve .
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/mayi/privileged-executor
Separate privileged command executor managed by systemd.
.PP
The package enables
.B mayi-broker.service
and
.BR mayi-privileged-executor.service .
The interactive approval console is provided by
.BR mayi-approve .
.SH EXAMPLES
Submit a synchronous request:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi /usr/bin/id
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print literal shell metacharacters without invoking a shell:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi /usr/bin/printf '%s\\n' 'literal; characters'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Explicitly request a shell (review carefully before approval):
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi /bin/sh -c 'printf "%s\\n" hello'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Queue a command and wait for successful completion:
.PP
.nf
.RS
d1=$(mayi --queue /usr/bin/true)
d2=$(mayi --queue --after "$d1" /usr/bin/id)
mayi --wait --fail=0 "$d1" "$d2"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Allow one unsuccessful request while waiting for three jobs:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mayi --wait --fail=1 "$id1" "$id2" "$id3"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mayi-workflow (7)
